erface-spec.a1] section 8. It runs DUTs at N=8 and N=32 on one APB bus. Each DUT has a cycle-by-cycle reference model with its own decode, written independently, and a monitor compares every output on every edge. Directed checks use literal expected values and cover:\n- reset values;\n- every read/write register with partial strobes and bits above N;\n- gpio_out and gpio_oe;\n- DATA_IN being read only, with one-edge latency;\n- the PSLVERR policy, including aliasing addresses;\n- rise, fall, both-edge pulse, high and low detection;\n- edge fires once, and level re-sets after a clear;\n- clearing some pending bits only, and clearing under strobes;\n- a clear on the edge of a new detection;\n- the interrupt mask, and reset again.\nFailures report with $error, and one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict line prints before $finish.", "entry": "apb_gpio_tb"}], "tool_runs": [], "claims": ["The RTL is intended to be Verilator -Wall lint clean at N 8 and 32 without waivers. Not checked by me.", "The RTL is intended to synthesize in Yosys with no latches. Not checked by me.", "The testbench is intended to pass under Icarus Verilog and Verilator (--binary/--timing). I have not run it.", "The testbench's literal expected values were derived by hand from the spec's timing rules: a pin change before edge E is sampled on E and sets status on E+1. They have not been reconciled against a simulation run."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Things to know about this submission:\n- **No formal_spec.** The task's expected outputs are rtl_source and testbench, so I did not produce a formal_spec (.sby) file. The interface spec section 8 and the microarchitecture section 5 still ask for an unbounded proof with covers at N 8 and 32. That needs a follow-up task or a recorded decision before signoff.\n- **Assertions are unchecked.** The RTL contains a small ifdef FORMAL block of design-intent assertions, and no proof backs them.\n- **Testbench layout.** The testbench file holds two modules: apb_gpio_tb (the en